Done. `store::rename` is implemented and all 7 tests pass (5 new + 2 pre-existing).

Implementation in `rust/src/store.rs:17` — reads `{old_id}.note`, splits on the `\n---\n` separator (same as `load_all`), re-emits the file as `{new_id}.note` with `new_id`/`new_title` substituted and the pinned flag and body taken verbatim, then deletes the old file. Missing source file propagates the IO error (NotFound); a malformed file returns `InvalidData`. Writing the new file before removing the old one means a crash mid-rename can never lose data.

One deviation worth noting: the file format stores tags only implicitly (`load_all` re-derives them via `extract_tags` from the body), so "tags unchanged" is guaranteed by preserving the body byte-for-byte. The pinned state is the explicit third field and is preserved as-is.

Tests in `rust/tests/rename.rs` (tempdir-based, added `tempfile` as a dev-dependency):
- full rename: new id/title appear in `load_all`, body/tags/pinned preserved, `old_id` has no trace on disk, sibling note untouched
- pinned=true survives a rename
- same-id rename (title-only update) doesn't duplicate
- renaming a missing id errors
- title containing the `\n---\n` separator still round-trips correctly

